      "question": {
        ":question/id": "1d172643-ef77-5b31-90e2-e671024c9a29",
        ":question/problem": "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx={e}^{2t},\\,y={t}^{2},\\,0\\le t\\le 2.\n$$\n\nDetermine the value of the parameter $t$ at which the normal line to the curve is vertical.",
        ":question/worked-solution": "The normal line is vertical when the tangent to the curve is horizontal. Therefore, we require ${y}^{\u2032}(t)=0$ while ${x}^{\u2032}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n{x}^{\u2032}(t)=2{e}^{2t},\\,{y}^{\u2032}(t)=2t\n$$\n\nSolving ${y}^{\u2032}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n2t=0\\,\u27f9\\,t=0.\n$$\n\nNow, we note that\n\n$$\n{x}^{\u2032}(0)=2{e}^{0}=2\\ne 0,\\,\\,\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the normal to the curve is indeed vertical when $t=0.$",
        ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
        ":question/answer-fields": [
          {
