        ":question/id": "ccb7dad8-92eb-58b7-8a2a-a63672d19bfa",
        ":question/problem": "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx={t}^{2}-8t,\\,y=2{t}^{3}.\n$$\n\nDetermine the value of the parameter $t$ at which the normal line to the curve is horizontal. Do not round the answer.\n\nThe normal to the curve is horizontal at $t=$\n{{field-1}}.",
        ":question/worked-solution": "The normal line is horizontal when the tangent line to the curve is vertical. Therefore, we require ${x}^{\u2032}(t)=0$ while ${y}^{\u2032}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n{x}^{\u2032}(t)=2t-8,\\,{y}^{\u2032}(t)=6{t}^{2}\n$$\n\nSolving ${x}^{\u2032}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2t-8 & =0 \\\\ 2t & =8 \\\\ t & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we note that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{\u2032}(4) & =6{(4)}^{2}=96\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the normal to the curve is indeed horizontal when $t=4.$",
