      },
      "question": {
        ":question/id": "d6a268b8-1a37-5cc5-a0c8-d12ada121580",
        ":question/problem": "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(t-\\frac{\\pi }{3})+8,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(t-\\frac{\\pi }{3})-5,\\,0\\le t<2\\pi .\n$$\n\nDetermine the values of $t$ at which the normal to the curve is vertical. Do not round the answers.\n\nIn **ascending order**, the normal to the curve is vertical at $t=$\n{{field-1}}\nand $t=$\n{{field-2}}.",
        ":question/worked-solution": "The normal is vertical if the tangent is horizontal. Therefore, we require ${y}^{\u2032}(t)=0$ while ${x}^{\u2032}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n{x}^{\u2032}(t)=-4\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(t-\\frac{\\pi }{3}),\\,{y}^{\u2032}(t)=4\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(t-\\frac{\\pi }{3})\n$$\n\nSolving ${y}^{\u2032}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n4\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(t-\\frac{\\pi }{3})=0\\,\u27f9\\,t=\\frac{5\\pi }{6},\\frac{11\\pi }{6}.\n$$\n\nNow, we note that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{\u2032}(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}) & =-4\\ne 0, \\\\ {x}^{\u2032}(\\frac{11\\pi }{6}) & =4\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the normal to the curve is indeed vertical when $t=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}$ and $t=\\frac{11\\pi }{6}.$",
        ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
        ":question/answer-fields": [
          {
            ":answer-field/id": "a224d7b1-7893-5b0a-911d-f49a4ea907ae",
