    "record": {
      "question": {
        ":question/id": "e5eafad9-4c2d-5469-bbd9-3cd0fcca968a",
        ":question/problem": "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}\u20614t-11,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}\u20614t+5,\\,0\\le t<\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nDetermine the values of $t$ at which the normal to the curve is horizontal.\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked.\n\n- **I.** $t=0$\n- **II.** $t=\\frac{\\pi}{6}$\n- **III.** $t=\\frac{\\pi}{4}$\n- **IV.** $t=\\frac{\\pi}{3}$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "The normal is horizontal if the tangent is vertical. Therefore, we require ${x}^{\u2032}(t)=0$ while ${y}^{\u2032}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{\u2032}(t)=-8\\operatorname{sin}\u20614t,\\,{y}^{\u2032}(t)=12\\operatorname{cos}\u20614t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving ${x}^{\u2032}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n-8\\operatorname{sin}\u20614t=0\\,\u27f9\\,t=0,\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nNow, we note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{\u2032}(0) & =12\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(0)=12\\ne 0, \\\\ {y}^{\u2032}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =12\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(\\pi )=-12\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the normal to the curve is indeed horizontal when $t=0$ and $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$",
        ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
